Original commit body from f33a6d8b035f4524879bfdbc75c4efbfdd339a3a:

Drains both of swift's container-member parity holes AND fixes a
structural gap underneath them that no analyzer change could reach.

ANALYZER. The Swift analyzer emitted an enum's methods and computed
properties (`Color.label`, `Color.name`) but never its CASES, and emitted
nothing at all inside a `protocol`. The enum half is a worked example of
why "the container has a member" is not a safe check: an enum carrying
one method already looked healthy on a span-nesting probe while every one
of its cases was invisible. Cases become kind="field"; a
protocol_function_declaration becomes kind="method"; a
protocol_property_declaration becomes kind="property" rather than
"field", because `{ get }` is a computed-access contract and never
storage -- the same field-vs-property distinction the analyzer already
draws for stored and computed members of a struct.

`case green, blue` is a SINGLE enum_entry carrying TWO simple_identifier
children, so emission iterates identifiers, not entries. A per-entry loop
would have silently dropped every case after the first comma -- the kind
of partial-recall bug that leaves the container looking populated. An
associated-value case (`case rgb(Int, Int)`) keeps its types in a sibling
enum_type_parameters node, so the direct-child scan reads the bare case
name and nothing else.

LINKER + SLICER, which is the part that mattered. After the analyzer
change the isolated-analyzer tests were green and the parity column read
green -- and the end-to-end reverse slice from a protocol was UNCHANGED
at 2 nodes. In the full pipeline the requirements existed and were
correctly named (`Drawable.draw`, `Drawable.area`) while the protocol
scored contains_out=0, with a sibling enum and struct in the same file
each rooting 3 members. Cause: `protocol` was absent from
linkers.containment.CONTAINER_KINDS and from the slicer's own container
set, so Swift/Obj-C protocol containment was structurally impossible
regardless of emission. It is a registered Symbol.kind emitted by both
swift.py and objc.py and belongs in both sets for exactly the reason
`interface` does. With it added:

  slice --entry Drawable --language swift --reverse   2 nodes -> 4
  slice --entry Color    --language swift --reverse   2 nodes -> 4

This is the concrete instance of a standing hazard: a component correct
at unit level can be INERT in the pipeline, and an emission-parity column
that measures one analyzer in isolation is blind to it by construction.
Found only by re-running the end-to-end repro instead of certifying from
the passing unit test.

STALE COMMENTS. Checking for analogues turned up three comments naming
`service` as a container kind; none was true. The linker's module
docstring listed "services", its CONTAINER_KINDS comment said "service
for proto (contains RPCs)", and slice.py cited containment's set as one
that "adds `service`, `message`" -- using that as the stated
justification for the slicer's set being narrower. The ADR-0027 Cluster-D
fold made `interface` + meta["framework_role"]="service" canonical years
earlier and proto.py emits exactly that today, so no producer emits
kind="service" at all. Corrected in all three places rather than left to
send the next reader through the same audit.

Ratchet: both swift cells XPASSed and FAILED the run until removed from
KNOWN_HOLES here. `emits_abstract_members` is now FULLY DRAINED -- every
applicable cell is a hard lock again. Remaining: enum members for java
and csharp.

